n't have to be that way for  us and I think actually is any worse  than it was in Roman times so the  question is how do we understand being  on one's own and so he's going to draw  this vital distinction between being  forlorn or being lonely and being alone  he says forlorn this means ultimately  being without help or resources the  greek word for this is bois de tous  that it's coming from a verb meaning to  sort of rush to the aid of somebody else  it's used in a lot of combats and and  military discussions but the idea is  that the person who feels like they're  they're sad and lonely because they're  on their own they they feel that way in  part because they can't rely on anybody  else in addition to that there's you  know so maybe some examples here that he  gives and some other qualifications he  says that this often happens when there  is a loss that the person endures of a  relative a close relative like a father  or a brother or a son or a close friend  somebody who one relied upon he actually  says a friend with whom one shared of  that meaning that there was a kind of  intimacy and closeness there losing a  spouse would be a great example and we  can feel this this sense of being on  their own you know all by herself even  if we are with others he gives some some  examples of being in a crowd in Rome he  says if we could be in the city of Rome  which is the largest city in the world  that he knows about with large crowds  meeting us on the streets and so many  other people living in the same house  with us and sometimes even though we  have a multitude of slaves or servants  people can handle all of that stuff all  those people around them and yet feel  like they're totally alone  that they're totally on their own  because they can't rely on any of them  to be there with what they need now from  a stoic perspective what does this mean  they are looking for something from  these other people perhaps reasonably  perhaps unreasonably that that's still a  q